/-!
# Baer–Suzuki theorem

This file pins the target statement of the Baer–Suzuki theorem in the exact
shape needed by Glauberman, *A Characteristic Subgroup of a p-Stable Group*,
Lemma 6.1 (see `refs/glauberman-p-stable.tex` L1599–L1613, and the pinned
statement `Glauberman.Lemma6_1.lemma6_1`):

> **Theorem (Baer–Suzuki).**  Let `x` be a `p`-element of a finite group `G`.
> If `x` and every conjugate `g * x * g⁻¹` generate a `p`-subgroup of `G`,
> then `x ∈ O_p(G)`.

Sources: R. Baer, *Engelsche Elemente Noetherscher Gruppen*, Math. Ann. 133
(1957), 256–270 (case `p = 2`; local copy `books/baer-engelsche.pdf` —
Satz L/R and Satz N give the Engel route: a finite group generated by its
Engel elements is nilpotent); M. Suzuki, *Finite groups in which the
centralizer of any element of order 2 is 2-closed*, Ann. of Math. (2) 82
(1965), 191–212 (general `p`).  Elementary proofs: Alperin–Lyons, *On
conjugacy classes of p-elements*, J. Algebra 19 (1971), 536–537; Huppert–
Blackburn, *Finite Groups III*, p. 298.  Suzuki's 1961 construction of the
weakly closed subgroup used in the proof is in *Finite groups with nilpotent
centralizers*, Trans. Amer. Math. Soc. 99 (1961), 425–470, Prop. 2
(local copies `books/suzuki-centralizer.pdf` and `books/suzuki-nilpotent.pdf`).

The classical conclusion is that the normal closure `⟨x^G⟩` is itself a
`p`-subgroup; the statement below is the equivalent `O_p`-membership form
that `Glauberman.Lemma6_1` consumes.  The `p`-element hypothesis is included
explicitly to match the pinned lemma; it is in fact derivable from the pair
hypothesis by taking `g = 1`.

## Proof route (minimal counterexample)

1. Reduce to `G = ⟨x^G⟩`: if the normal closure `X = ⟨x^G⟩` were not a
   `p`-group, choose `G` minimal with `X` not a `p`-group; the pair
   hypothesis passes to proper quotients, so `X/N` is a `p`-group for every
   minimal normal subgroup `N` of `X`.
2. Structure of the counterexample (Guralnick–Robinson, *Normal p-subgroups
   of finite groups*, J. London Math. Soc. (2) 8 (1974); Aschbacher, *Finite
   Group Theory*, §31): `G` has a unique minimal normal subgroup
   `M = S₁ × ⋯ × Sₙ` with the `Sᵢ` nonabelian simple, and `G/M` is a
   `p`-group.  Conjugation by `x` permutes the factors in cycles of
   `p`-power length.
3. Contradiction: choose a nontrivial `s ∈ S₁`; combining a conjugate of `s`
   lying in a different `x`-orbit factor with an appropriate conjugate of
   `x` produces a subgroup containing `s` and an element outside `M`, whose
   image in `G/M` is a nontrivial `q`-element for a prime `q ≠ p` — or
   directly a subgroup not of `p`-power order — contradicting the pair
   hypothesis.

The exact core lemmas will be extracted as helpers and recorded in
`tasks/baer-suzuki.md`.

## State

Proved by reduction to the repository's Alperin--Lyons theorem and wired into
`Glauberman.Lemma6_1`.
-/

namespace CFSG.BaerSuzuki

/-- **Baer–Suzuki theorem** (O_p form, as consumed by Glauberman Lemma 6.1).

If `x` is a `p`-element of the finite group `G` and, for every `g ∈ G`, the
subgroup generated by `x` and its conjugate `g * x * g⁻¹` is a `p`-group,
then `x ∈ O_p(G)` (`pCore p G`). -/
public theorem baer_suzuki {p : ℕ} [Fact p.Prime] {G : Type*} [Group G] [Finite G]
    (x : G) (hx : IsPGroup p (Subgroup.zpowers x))
    (hall : ∀ g : G, IsPGroup p (Subgroup.closure ({x, g * x * g⁻¹} : Set G))) :
    x ∈ pCore p G := by
  have hx_p : IsPElement (p := p) x := by
    rcases IsPGroup.iff_card.mp hx with ⟨n, hn⟩
    rw [Nat.card_zpowers] at hn
    exact ⟨n, hn⟩
  refine BenderSuzuki.gorenstein_3_8_2_conjugacy_class_le_pCore hx_p ?_ x ?_
  · intro y hy
    rcases hy with ⟨g, rfl⟩
    exact hall g
  · exact ⟨1, by simp⟩

end CFSG.BaerSuzuki
